/*****************************************************************************
    jitJU -- RUNG JU OF THE jitLadder. 2026-08-03.

        + THE UNARY OPERATORS (++ and --), in-place on data nodes.

    Each rung is the previous plus ONE construct. JU adds the unary family,
    which until today could not be jitted at all: all three unary POPs
    (jitInc/jitDec/jitNeg) exited 139 inside jitEmitUnary on a null
    target->jitData, and had done since the 2026-06-30 unified-emit pivot
    folded out jitXP and moved seeding to runOP's gate.

    ⚠ THE CAUSE, MEASURED 2026-08-03 (it was `inferred` in the corpus for four
    days, and the corpus said so): runOP's seed gate read

        if jitting && op.isOperator

    but every unary operator is registered `unary ruleMethod=` (incant/setup)
    -- isUnary and isMethod, NOT isOperator -- so dispatch took runOP's
    `or op.isMethod` arm and NO OPERAND WAS EVER SEEDED. jitEmitUnary
    dereferences target->jitData unconditionally, so the miss was a SIGSEGV
    rather than a wrong answer.

    Probe at the crash frame, which is what turned inferred into verified:
        target            = 0x...f00   (a healthy field, getText() == "13")
        target->jitData   = nil        <- the deref, fault address 0x8
        gJitSeeded.size() = 0          <- ZERO operands seeded, the quantity
        gJitBuilder / gJitCurrentFn / gJitResultSlot  all non-null
    The last line is what eliminated the rival hypothesis. The emit context was
    fully set up; it was ONLY the seeding. The gate now reads

        if jitting && (op.isOperator || op.isUnary)

    isUnary is the precise gate. Widening to isMethod would have seeded an
    operand for every rule method in the language.

    ============================================================================
    WHY THE VALUES ARE 11 AND 31, and why the wrong answers are diagnostic
    (the ladder's own rule: a rung must NAME its failure mode from the value):

        juOut = juIn;  ++juOut;  ++juOut;  --juOut;     net +1

        11 / 31   correct
        10 / 30   BOTH unary ops no-oped (the pre-fix emit path, had it not crashed)
        12 / 32   `--` no-oped, `++` worked
         9 / 29   `++` no-oped, `--` worked
    Two increments and one decrement, rather than one of each, exactly so that
    ++ and -- cannot both fail and still land on the right answer.

    ⚠ INJECTIVITY (the J7 lesson): fire 1 and fire 2 must differ as ANSWERS,
    not merely as inputs. 11 != 31.
    ============================================================================

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitLadder);
define
    juIn;
    juOut;
    /*  The rung: an assign to establish the operand, then the unary family
        applied in place. ++ and -- both write back to the operand's slot, so
        this also exercises jitEmitUnary's store-back leg, not just its
        arithmetic.  */
    jitJU code={ juOut = juIn; ++juOut; ++juOut; --juOut; };
    ;

juIn  = 10;
juOut = 0;
print "JU fire 1 setup : juIn =" juIn:;
testing(jitJU);
print "JU fire 1 result: juOut =" juOut " (want 11)":;

/*  ⚠ NO RECOMPILE BELOW THIS LINE. Only the input changes. If fire 2 returns
    11 the operand was FOLDED at compile time and fire 1 proved nothing --
    which is the specific risk here, because under jitting the interpreter runs
    the body for real at emit time and ++ MUTATES IN PLACE.  */
juIn  = 30;
juOut = 0;
print "JU fire 2 setup : juIn =" juIn " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JU fire 2 result: juOut =" juOut " (want 31; 11 = FOLDED; 0 = never fired)":;

/*  ⚠ THE ORACLE'S TESTIMONY, captured LAST and that order is LOAD-BEARING --
    calling the action interpreted BEFORE testing() consumes its isCoded state
    and testing() then silently routes to jitRunIfTest instead of
    jitRunAction (measured on J3, 2026-07-31).  */
juIn  = 10;
juOut = 0;
jitJU();
print "JU interpreted  : juOut =" juOut " (the oracle -- want 11)":;

print "JU SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
